{"id":"h243","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"A racing car traveling in a circular path maintains a constant speed of 120 miles per hour. If the radius of the circular track is 1000 ft, what if any acceleration does the center of gravity of the car exhibit?\nIs the correct answer \"0 ft/s^2 (no acceleration)\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The proposed answer of 0 ft/s² is incorrect.","confidence":0.98,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5394,"phi":2.9584},{"text":"Constant speed does not imply constant velocity, because the direction keeps changing around the curve.","confidence":0.98,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5394,"phi":2.9884},{"text":"Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, so the car accelerates.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":3.0185},{"text":"120 mi/h converts to 176 ft/s.","confidence":0.97,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.5237,"phi":2.9313},{"text":"Centripetal acceleration is v²/r, about 31 ft/s² (about 0.96 g), directed toward the center.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":2.9313},{"text":"Because the speedometer reading is constant, there is no tangential acceleration, which is the only sense in which the motion is constant.","confidence":0.6,"lean":1,"theta":0.9425,"phi":1.4756},{"text":"Any change in velocity, in magnitude or direction, counts as acceleration.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":3.033},{"text":"The center of gravity moves on a circular path, so it requires a net inward force that produces inward acceleration.","confidence":0.93,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":3.0213},{"text":"The vector nature of velocity is the deciding consideration.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.0406}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.85,